1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
When a giant ship sits in dry

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:05,214
dock, you can see something strange below

3
00:00:05,214 --> 00:00:06,257
the waterline.

4
00:00:06,437 --> 00:00:07,760
At the very front,

5
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,038
a huge rounded bulb sticks out,

6
00:00:10,038 --> 00:00:12,022
like the nose of a dolphin.

7
00:00:12,202 --> 00:00:13,918
You never see it when the ship

8
00:00:13,918 --> 00:00:14,547
is sailing.

9
00:00:14,727 --> 00:00:17,186
But it saves enormous amounts of fuel

10
00:00:17,186 --> 00:00:18,117
on every trip.

11
00:00:18,297 --> 00:00:21,069
Today, ten parts of a ship,

12
00:00:21,069 --> 00:00:23,533
and why each one exists.

13
00:00:23,883 --> 00:00:25,810
A big ship is one of the

14
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:28,620
most efficient machines ever built.

15
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:32,170
It can carry thousands of containers across

16
00:00:32,170 --> 00:00:35,070
an ocean, burning less fuel per tonne

17
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:36,951
than a truck or a train.

18
00:00:37,131 --> 00:00:40,167
That efficiency comes from parts most people

19
00:00:40,167 --> 00:00:42,652
never see, many of them hidden under

20
00:00:42,652 --> 00:00:43,342
the water.

21
00:00:43,691 --> 00:00:46,692
As always, every fact here comes from

22
00:00:46,692 --> 00:00:47,909
a named source,

23
00:00:47,909 --> 00:00:52,613
maritime organisations, shipbuilders and naval references,

24
00:00:52,613 --> 00:00:55,046
all listed in the description.

25
00:00:55,226 --> 00:00:57,014
Let's start with the bulb.

26
00:00:57,363 --> 00:00:59,432
Why do ships have a big bulb

27
00:00:59,432 --> 00:01:00,614
under the front?

28
00:01:00,794 --> 00:01:02,118
To fight waves.

29
00:01:02,298 --> 00:01:03,568
As a ship moves,

30
00:01:03,568 --> 00:01:06,345
it pushes water aside and creates a

31
00:01:06,345 --> 00:01:08,091
large wave at the bow.

32
00:01:08,271 --> 00:01:10,610
Making that wave costs energy,

33
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:11,545
a lot of it.

34
00:01:11,725 --> 00:01:14,971
The bulb, sitting just below the surface,

35
00:01:14,971 --> 00:01:17,108
creates its own small wave,

36
00:01:17,108 --> 00:01:19,562
slightly ahead of the main one.

37
00:01:19,911 --> 00:01:22,268
If the bulb is shaped and placed

38
00:01:22,268 --> 00:01:25,066
correctly, its wave meets the main bow

39
00:01:25,066 --> 00:01:26,317
wave out of step,

40
00:01:26,317 --> 00:01:28,526
with a crest meeting a trough.

41
00:01:28,706 --> 00:01:30,912
The two partly cancel each other.

42
00:01:31,092 --> 00:01:33,347
The ship makes a smaller wave,

43
00:01:33,347 --> 00:01:35,527
and needs less power to move.

44
00:01:35,707 --> 00:01:38,533
On large ships at their design speed,

45
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:40,901
the saving can be well over ten

46
00:01:40,901 --> 00:01:42,429
percent of the fuel.

47
00:01:42,779 --> 00:01:44,184
There is a catch.

48
00:01:44,364 --> 00:01:47,204
The bulb works best at one speed

49
00:01:47,204 --> 00:01:49,600
and one depth in the water.

50
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:53,487
When shipping companies started sailing slower to

51
00:01:53,487 --> 00:01:56,059
save fuel, some found that the old

52
00:01:56,059 --> 00:01:57,799
bulbs no longer helped,

53
00:01:57,799 --> 00:01:59,312
and even added drag.

54
00:01:59,492 --> 00:02:02,114
Some ships had their bows cut off

55
00:02:02,114 --> 00:02:04,419
and rebuilt with a new shape,

56
00:02:04,419 --> 00:02:06,644
just to match the new speed.

57
00:02:06,993 --> 00:02:09,757
Why are ship propellers so huge?

58
00:02:09,937 --> 00:02:11,187
Because a big,

59
00:02:11,187 --> 00:02:14,672
slow propeller is more efficient than a

60
00:02:14,672 --> 00:02:16,102
small, fast one.

61
00:02:16,282 --> 00:02:18,837
It pushes a large amount of water

62
00:02:18,837 --> 00:02:22,322
backwards, gently, rather than a small amount

63
00:02:22,322 --> 00:02:23,097
very fast.

64
00:02:23,277 --> 00:02:26,372
The propeller of a large container ship

65
00:02:26,372 --> 00:02:29,150
can be almost ten metres across and

66
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:31,531
weigh over one hundred tonnes.

67
00:02:31,881 --> 00:02:35,541
Spinning slowly also avoids a problem called

68
00:02:35,541 --> 00:02:36,456
cavitation.

69
00:02:36,636 --> 00:02:39,537
When a propeller blade spins too fast,

70
00:02:39,537 --> 00:02:41,905
the pressure on the back of the

71
00:02:41,905 --> 00:02:44,425
blade drops so low that the water

72
00:02:44,425 --> 00:02:46,257
boils into tiny bubbles,

73
00:02:46,257 --> 00:02:48,014
even in cold sea water.

74
00:02:48,194 --> 00:02:50,122
When those bubbles collapse,

75
00:02:50,122 --> 00:02:52,669
they hit the metal like tiny hammers,

76
00:02:52,669 --> 00:02:54,666
and they can eat holes in the

77
00:02:54,666 --> 00:02:55,148
blades.

78
00:02:55,497 --> 00:02:58,052
That is why giant propellers turn at

79
00:02:58,052 --> 00:03:01,032
around one hundred revolutions per minute,

80
00:03:01,032 --> 00:03:03,160
slower than a washing machine.

81
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:05,907
Each one is cast in one piece

82
00:03:05,907 --> 00:03:09,004
from a bronze alloy and polished by

83
00:03:09,004 --> 00:03:09,447
hand.

84
00:03:09,627 --> 00:03:12,257
A small scratch or bend can cost

85
00:03:12,257 --> 00:03:13,818
fuel on every trip,

86
00:03:13,818 --> 00:03:15,873
for the life of the ship.

87
00:03:16,223 --> 00:03:18,098
Why do many ships have a round

88
00:03:18,098 --> 00:03:20,723
tunnel going straight through the front of

89
00:03:20,723 --> 00:03:21,285
the hull?

90
00:03:21,465 --> 00:03:23,352
It holds a bow thruster,

91
00:03:23,352 --> 00:03:25,947
a propeller that pushes sideways.

92
00:03:26,127 --> 00:03:29,199
A big ship's main propeller and rudder

93
00:03:29,199 --> 00:03:31,706
only work well when the ship is

94
00:03:31,706 --> 00:03:32,919
moving forward.

95
00:03:33,099 --> 00:03:34,337
At very low speed,

96
00:03:34,337 --> 00:03:35,232
in a harbour,

97
00:03:35,232 --> 00:03:37,503
they cannot turn the front of the

98
00:03:37,503 --> 00:03:37,847
ship.

99
00:03:38,197 --> 00:03:40,241
The bow thruster solves that.

100
00:03:40,421 --> 00:03:42,951
By pushing water to the left or

101
00:03:42,951 --> 00:03:45,727
right, it swings the bow sideways,

102
00:03:45,727 --> 00:03:48,339
without the ship moving forward.

103
00:03:48,519 --> 00:03:51,460
Many ships have thrusters at the stern

104
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:51,770
too.

105
00:03:51,950 --> 00:03:55,242
Together, they let a ship slide sideways

106
00:03:55,242 --> 00:03:56,312
into a berth,

107
00:03:56,312 --> 00:03:59,276
without needing tugboats every time.

108
00:03:59,625 --> 00:04:02,446
The thruster tunnel is usually marked on

109
00:04:02,446 --> 00:04:04,350
the side of the hull with a

110
00:04:04,350 --> 00:04:06,535
sign, a circle with an arrow or

111
00:04:06,535 --> 00:04:08,298
the letters for thruster.

112
00:04:08,478 --> 00:04:11,283
It warns small boats and tugboats to

113
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:14,633
stay clear, because the thruster can create

114
00:04:14,633 --> 00:04:17,516
a strong current that pulls or pushes

115
00:04:17,516 --> 00:04:17,905
them.

116
00:04:18,085 --> 00:04:21,446
A small sign for a powerful hidden

117
00:04:21,446 --> 00:04:21,940
part.

118
00:04:22,289 --> 00:04:23,996
Why is there a circle with a

119
00:04:23,996 --> 00:04:26,129
line through it painted on the side

120
00:04:26,129 --> 00:04:26,678
of ships?

121
00:04:26,858 --> 00:04:28,460
It is the load line,

122
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:30,863
also called the Plimsoll line.

123
00:04:31,043 --> 00:04:33,263
It shows how deep a ship can

124
00:04:33,263 --> 00:04:35,165
safely sit in the water.

125
00:04:35,345 --> 00:04:37,741
If the water rises above the line,

126
00:04:37,741 --> 00:04:39,362
the ship is overloaded,

127
00:04:39,362 --> 00:04:41,405
and it could sink in a storm.

128
00:04:41,755 --> 00:04:44,561
It is named after Samuel Plimsoll,

129
00:04:44,561 --> 00:04:48,026
a British politician who campaigned in the

130
00:04:48,026 --> 00:04:51,657
eighteen seventies against overloaded ships,

131
00:04:51,657 --> 00:04:55,122
which owners sometimes sent to sea knowing

132
00:04:55,122 --> 00:04:56,442
they might sink.

133
00:04:56,622 --> 00:04:59,141
The law made the mark mandatory.

134
00:04:59,321 --> 00:05:00,683
Next to the circle,

135
00:05:00,683 --> 00:05:03,478
a ladder of short lines shows different

136
00:05:03,478 --> 00:05:06,345
limits for different waters and seasons.

137
00:05:06,695 --> 00:05:09,637
The ladder exists because water is not

138
00:05:09,637 --> 00:05:10,643
all the same.

139
00:05:10,823 --> 00:05:13,762
Fresh water is less dense than sea

140
00:05:13,762 --> 00:05:16,528
water, so a ship sinks deeper in

141
00:05:16,528 --> 00:05:17,220
a river.

142
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,767
Cold water is denser than warm water.

143
00:05:20,947 --> 00:05:23,400
And winter seas are rougher,

144
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:25,591
so ships must carry less.

145
00:05:25,771 --> 00:05:28,833
One painted mark keeps all these rules

146
00:05:28,833 --> 00:05:29,881
in one place.

147
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:32,342
Why is a ship's rudder placed right

148
00:05:32,342 --> 00:05:33,609
behind the propeller?

149
00:05:33,789 --> 00:05:37,078
Because the propeller blows a fast stream

150
00:05:37,078 --> 00:05:39,164
of water straight onto it.

151
00:05:39,344 --> 00:05:42,396
A rudder works by turning that flowing

152
00:05:42,396 --> 00:05:45,126
water, pushing the stern sideways.

153
00:05:45,306 --> 00:05:46,797
The faster the water,

154
00:05:46,797 --> 00:05:48,360
the stronger the push.

155
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:49,980
Behind the propeller,

156
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:52,450
the rudder gets the fastest water on

157
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:53,067
the ship.

158
00:05:53,417 --> 00:05:55,879
This matters most at low speed.

159
00:05:56,059 --> 00:05:58,530
When the ship is moving slowly,

160
00:05:58,530 --> 00:06:01,321
the water around the hull is almost

161
00:06:01,321 --> 00:06:04,032
still, and a rudder in still water

162
00:06:04,032 --> 00:06:05,068
does nothing.

163
00:06:05,248 --> 00:06:07,433
But if the propeller is turning,

164
00:06:07,433 --> 00:06:09,959
it still blows water over the rudder,

165
00:06:09,959 --> 00:06:11,529
and the ship can steer.

166
00:06:11,709 --> 00:06:14,649
Captains use short bursts of power to

167
00:06:14,649 --> 00:06:16,318
turn in tight spaces.

168
00:06:16,668 --> 00:06:19,310
A large ship's rudder is the size

169
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:20,591
of a house wall,

170
00:06:20,591 --> 00:06:23,553
and it is moved by powerful hydraulic

171
00:06:23,553 --> 00:06:24,273
machines.

172
00:06:24,453 --> 00:06:28,226
Even so, a giant ship turns slowly.

173
00:06:28,406 --> 00:06:31,228
It can take several kilometres to stop

174
00:06:31,228 --> 00:06:32,714
or make a full turn.

175
00:06:32,894 --> 00:06:34,979
That is why ships have to plan

176
00:06:34,979 --> 00:06:37,412
every move long before a harbour or

177
00:06:37,412 --> 00:06:38,594
a narrow channel.

178
00:06:38,943 --> 00:06:42,330
Why do some ship propellers spin inside

179
00:06:42,330 --> 00:06:42,937
a ring?

180
00:06:43,117 --> 00:06:45,479
The ring is called a nozzle,

181
00:06:45,479 --> 00:06:46,744
or Kort nozzle,

182
00:06:46,744 --> 00:06:49,950
after the German engineer Ludwig Kort,

183
00:06:49,950 --> 00:06:51,384
who developed it.

184
00:06:51,564 --> 00:06:53,493
It is shaped like a short,

185
00:06:53,493 --> 00:06:55,942
thick tube, with a curved inside,

186
00:06:55,942 --> 00:06:58,390
like the cross section of a wing.

187
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:01,052
At low speed and high power,

188
00:07:01,052 --> 00:07:03,777
a propeller loses a lot of energy

189
00:07:03,777 --> 00:07:05,923
at the tips of its blades,

190
00:07:05,923 --> 00:07:08,400
where water slips around them.

191
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:10,078
The ring stops that,

192
00:07:10,078 --> 00:07:13,375
and guides more water through the propeller.

193
00:07:13,555 --> 00:07:16,244
The ring itself is also shaped to

194
00:07:16,244 --> 00:07:17,955
produce extra thrust.

195
00:07:18,135 --> 00:07:21,106
Together, they can push much harder than

196
00:07:21,106 --> 00:07:22,442
an open propeller.

197
00:07:22,792 --> 00:07:24,943
That is why you see nozzles on

198
00:07:24,943 --> 00:07:28,313
tugboats, fishing trawlers and other ships that

199
00:07:28,313 --> 00:07:30,536
need to pull heavy loads at low

200
00:07:30,536 --> 00:07:30,966
speed.

201
00:07:31,146 --> 00:07:33,717
A tugboat is not built to go

202
00:07:33,717 --> 00:07:34,176
fast.

203
00:07:34,356 --> 00:07:36,508
It is built to push and pull

204
00:07:36,508 --> 00:07:37,507
a giant ship,

205
00:07:37,507 --> 00:07:40,350
and the nozzle gives it extra pulling

206
00:07:40,350 --> 00:07:41,810
power for its size.

207
00:07:42,159 --> 00:07:45,089
Why is the anchor chain more important

208
00:07:45,089 --> 00:07:46,861
than the anchor itself?

209
00:07:47,041 --> 00:07:49,431
Because most of the holding comes from

210
00:07:49,431 --> 00:07:50,060
the chain.

211
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:52,351
A ship lets out much more chain

212
00:07:52,351 --> 00:07:54,257
than the depth of the water.

213
00:07:54,437 --> 00:07:56,933
The heavy chain lies along the sea

214
00:07:56,933 --> 00:07:59,356
floor, and it pulls on the anchor

215
00:07:59,356 --> 00:08:02,512
horizontally, so the anchor digs in instead

216
00:08:02,512 --> 00:08:03,981
of being lifted out.

217
00:08:04,330 --> 00:08:06,792
The weight of the chain also acts

218
00:08:06,792 --> 00:08:07,837
like a spring.

219
00:08:08,017 --> 00:08:10,263
When a wave or the wind pulls

220
00:08:10,263 --> 00:08:12,819
the ship, the chain lifts off the

221
00:08:12,819 --> 00:08:14,059
seabed a little,

222
00:08:14,059 --> 00:08:15,608
absorbing the shock,

223
00:08:15,608 --> 00:08:17,699
and then settles back down.

224
00:08:17,879 --> 00:08:20,323
If the chain were short and straight,

225
00:08:20,323 --> 00:08:22,701
every pull would yank the anchor out

226
00:08:22,701 --> 00:08:23,824
of the sea floor.

227
00:08:24,173 --> 00:08:26,813
A large ship's anchor chain is made

228
00:08:26,813 --> 00:08:29,301
of links that can weigh more than

229
00:08:29,301 --> 00:08:31,186
a hundred kilograms each.

230
00:08:31,366 --> 00:08:34,064
The chain runs into a locker deep

231
00:08:34,064 --> 00:08:35,290
inside the bow.

232
00:08:35,470 --> 00:08:37,622
When the anchor is dropped,

233
00:08:37,622 --> 00:08:39,695
it roars out in a cloud of

234
00:08:39,695 --> 00:08:40,811
rust and dust.

235
00:08:40,991 --> 00:08:43,505
One of the oldest tricks in shipping

236
00:08:43,505 --> 00:08:45,321
is still all about weight.

237
00:08:45,671 --> 00:08:48,502
Why are there small metal blocks fixed

238
00:08:48,502 --> 00:08:50,141
to the hull of a ship?

239
00:08:50,321 --> 00:08:52,838
They are sacrificial anodes,

240
00:08:52,838 --> 00:08:55,894
usually made of zinc or aluminium.

241
00:08:56,074 --> 00:08:58,675
Steel in salt water rusts,

242
00:08:58,675 --> 00:09:02,076
through a slow electrical process.

243
00:09:02,256 --> 00:09:05,051
Two different metals in sea water act

244
00:09:05,051 --> 00:09:06,184
like a battery,

245
00:09:06,184 --> 00:09:09,280
and the more active metal corrodes first.

246
00:09:09,630 --> 00:09:12,891
Zinc and aluminium are more active than

247
00:09:12,891 --> 00:09:13,392
steel.

248
00:09:13,572 --> 00:09:15,652
So when they are attached to the

249
00:09:15,652 --> 00:09:18,251
hull, they corrode instead of the steel.

250
00:09:18,431 --> 00:09:19,920
They slowly dissolve,

251
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:21,480
giving up their metal,

252
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:23,679
while the hull stays protected.

253
00:09:23,859 --> 00:09:26,262
That is why they are called sacrificial.

254
00:09:26,442 --> 00:09:28,497
They die so the ship does not.

255
00:09:28,846 --> 00:09:31,301
Anodes are placed near the propeller,

256
00:09:31,301 --> 00:09:34,087
the rudder and other parts where different

257
00:09:34,087 --> 00:09:36,872
metals meet, like the bronze propeller and

258
00:09:36,872 --> 00:09:37,867
the steel hull.

259
00:09:38,047 --> 00:09:40,535
They are checked and replaced in dry

260
00:09:40,535 --> 00:09:40,880
dock.

261
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:43,823
A few cheap blocks of zinc protect

262
00:09:43,823 --> 00:09:46,262
a ship worth tens of millions.

263
00:09:46,611 --> 00:09:48,538
Why does the bridge of a ship

264
00:09:48,538 --> 00:09:50,598
stick out past the sides of the

265
00:09:50,598 --> 00:09:50,930
hull?

266
00:09:51,110 --> 00:09:54,071
These extensions are called bridge wings.

267
00:09:54,251 --> 00:09:56,448
From the middle of the bridge,

268
00:09:56,448 --> 00:09:59,012
the officers cannot see the side of

269
00:09:59,012 --> 00:10:00,111
their own ship,

270
00:10:00,111 --> 00:10:01,942
or how close it is to the

271
00:10:01,942 --> 00:10:02,308
dock.

272
00:10:02,488 --> 00:10:04,414
From the end of a bridge wing,

273
00:10:04,414 --> 00:10:06,788
they can look straight down along the

274
00:10:06,788 --> 00:10:07,109
hull.

275
00:10:07,458 --> 00:10:10,332
During docking, the captain and the pilot

276
00:10:10,332 --> 00:10:12,365
walk out onto the wing to see

277
00:10:12,365 --> 00:10:14,608
the gap between the ship and the

278
00:10:14,608 --> 00:10:14,959
quay.

279
00:10:15,139 --> 00:10:17,900
Many bridge wings have a small control

280
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,733
station with the thrusters and engines,

281
00:10:20,733 --> 00:10:23,058
so the ship can be steered right

282
00:10:23,058 --> 00:10:23,858
from there.

283
00:10:24,038 --> 00:10:26,103
Some even have a glass floor to

284
00:10:26,103 --> 00:10:27,370
look straight down.

285
00:10:27,719 --> 00:10:29,611
On a giant container ship,

286
00:10:29,611 --> 00:10:32,231
the bridge can be hundreds of metres

287
00:10:32,231 --> 00:10:33,177
from the bow,

288
00:10:33,177 --> 00:10:35,942
and the view forward is partly blocked

289
00:10:35,942 --> 00:10:36,961
by containers.

290
00:10:37,141 --> 00:10:39,078
That is why the rules limit how

291
00:10:39,078 --> 00:10:40,766
much of the sea in front of

292
00:10:40,766 --> 00:10:42,203
the ship can be hidden.

293
00:10:42,383 --> 00:10:45,136
The bridge wings are the captain's eyes

294
00:10:45,136 --> 00:10:47,607
when the ship is closest to danger.

295
00:10:47,957 --> 00:10:50,735
Why do oil tankers have two hulls,

296
00:10:50,735 --> 00:10:52,451
one inside the other?

297
00:10:52,631 --> 00:10:54,546
To stop oil spills.

298
00:10:54,726 --> 00:10:57,417
A single hull is only one layer

299
00:10:57,417 --> 00:11:00,196
of steel between the oil and the

300
00:11:00,196 --> 00:11:00,543
sea.

301
00:11:00,723 --> 00:11:02,516
If the ship hits a rock,

302
00:11:02,516 --> 00:11:04,235
the hull can tear open,

303
00:11:04,235 --> 00:11:06,551
and the oil flows straight out.

304
00:11:06,731 --> 00:11:08,223
With a double hull,

305
00:11:08,223 --> 00:11:10,893
there is a space between the outer

306
00:11:10,893 --> 00:11:12,385
skin and the tanks.

307
00:11:12,734 --> 00:11:14,809
In nineteen eighty-nine,

308
00:11:14,809 --> 00:11:18,093
the tanker Exxon Valdez ran aground in

309
00:11:18,093 --> 00:11:21,117
Alaska and spilled a huge amount of

310
00:11:21,117 --> 00:11:24,661
crude oil, one of the worst environmental

311
00:11:24,661 --> 00:11:26,562
disasters of its time.

312
00:11:26,742 --> 00:11:30,078
After that, the United States passed laws

313
00:11:30,078 --> 00:11:31,950
requiring double hulls,

314
00:11:31,950 --> 00:11:35,611
and international rules soon followed for new

315
00:11:35,611 --> 00:11:36,262
tankers.

316
00:11:36,612 --> 00:11:38,081
In a minor grounding,

317
00:11:38,081 --> 00:11:40,529
the outer hull can be damaged while

318
00:11:40,529 --> 00:11:42,487
the inner hull stays intact.

319
00:11:42,667 --> 00:11:45,415
The space between them can also hold

320
00:11:45,415 --> 00:11:48,240
ballast water when the ship is empty.

321
00:11:48,420 --> 00:11:50,765
A double hull does not make spills

322
00:11:50,765 --> 00:11:53,316
impossible, but it has made them much

323
00:11:53,316 --> 00:11:54,144
less common.

324
00:11:54,324 --> 00:11:58,542
Sometimes one disaster changes how every ship

325
00:11:58,542 --> 00:11:59,386
is built.

326
00:11:59,735 --> 00:12:01,210
So here are the ten.

327
00:12:01,390 --> 00:12:04,165
A bulb that cancels the bow wave.

328
00:12:04,345 --> 00:12:06,388
A giant slow propeller.

329
00:12:06,568 --> 00:12:09,169
A sideways thruster for docking.

330
00:12:09,349 --> 00:12:12,135
A painted line against overloading.

331
00:12:12,315 --> 00:12:14,614
A rudder in the propeller's stream.

332
00:12:14,794 --> 00:12:17,313
A ring that adds pulling power.

333
00:12:17,493 --> 00:12:19,799
A chain that holds more than the

334
00:12:19,799 --> 00:12:20,303
anchor.

335
00:12:20,483 --> 00:12:23,664
Blocks of zinc that sacrifice themselves.

336
00:12:23,844 --> 00:12:26,607
Bridge wings for the captain's eyes.

337
00:12:26,787 --> 00:12:29,910
And a double hull against oil spills.

338
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:32,905
The biggest machines on Earth hide their

339
00:12:32,905 --> 00:12:35,021
cleverest parts under the water.

340
00:12:35,201 --> 00:12:38,207
If you want more machines explained part

341
00:12:38,207 --> 00:12:41,064
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

342
00:12:41,244 --> 00:12:43,199
And tell us in the comments which

343
00:12:43,199 --> 00:12:44,976
ship you want us to take apart

344
00:12:44,976 --> 00:12:45,272
next.
